#: conf.json:strings.method.lead
msgid "A package matters to the MoonBit ecosystem when other packages build on it. The score measures that reliance from the public registry, the way a journal impact factor measures how often a journal is cited."
msgstr ""

#: conf.json:strings.method.formulaLabel
msgid "Score formula"
msgstr ""

#: conf.json:strings.method.symbol
msgid "Symbol"
msgstr ""

#: conf.json:strings.method.weight
msgid "Weight"
msgstr ""

#: conf.json:strings.method.signals.title
msgid "Three signals"
msgstr ""

#: conf.json:strings.method.signals.body
msgid "Every package gets a score S from three counts and the age of its latest release t:"
msgstr ""

#: conf.json:strings.method.signals.dependents
msgid "Dependents: packages whose latest release declares this package as a dependency"
msgstr ""

#: conf.json:strings.method.signals.recent
msgid "New dependents: those whose dependency first appeared in the last 180 days"
msgstr ""

#: conf.json:strings.method.signals.downloads
msgid "Downloads reported by mooncakes.io"
msgstr ""

#: conf.json:strings.method.signals.log
msgid "The logarithm gives diminishing returns: going from 10 to 21 dependents adds as many points as going from 1000 to 2001. Dependents weigh most because a dependency is a deliberate decision; downloads also count automated builds."
msgstr ""

#: conf.json:strings.method.owners.title
msgid "Dependents of the same owner"
msgstr ""

#: conf.json:strings.method.owners.body
msgid "An author who splits one project into many packages adds a dependent for every package. Such dependents count a quarter each, as citation indicators discount self-citations, so a package ranks high only when other people build on it. The package page shows both counts."
msgstr ""

#: conf.json:strings.method.recency.title
msgid "Release recency"
msgstr ""

#: conf.json:strings.method.recency.body
msgid "The sum is multiplied by m(t): 1.12 for a release in the last 30 days, falling linearly to 0.88 at one year, and 0.88 after that. Maintained packages rank a little higher; an old package with many dependents still ranks high."
msgstr ""

#: conf.json:strings.method.grades.title
msgid "Grades"
msgstr ""

#: conf.json:strings.method.grades.body
msgid "Grades place a package within the registry rather than on an absolute scale, like journal quartiles. Packages are sorted by score and cut at fixed shares; equal scores share a position."
msgstr ""

#: conf.json:strings.method.grades.share
msgid "Share of the registry"
msgstr ""

#: conf.json:strings.method.grades.count
msgid "Packages"
msgstr ""

#: conf.json:strings.method.momentum.body
msgid "The score is computed again as it would have been 30 days ago, from the releases and dependencies published by then. A change counts only when it is at least 10 points and at least 10% of the earlier score, so neither a large package nor a tiny one changes label on noise."
msgstr ""

#: conf.json:strings.method.data.title
msgid "Data and updates"
msgstr ""

#: conf.json:strings.method.data.body
msgid "The rankings are rebuilt every day from the mooncakes.io registry index and its download counts. Yanked releases are ignored, and only the dependencies of each package's latest release count."
msgstr ""

#: conf.json:strings.method.data.current
msgid "This snapshot was computed on {date} and ranks {count} packages."
msgstr ""

#: conf.json:strings.method.limits.title
msgid "What the score does not say"
msgstr ""

#: conf.json:strings.method.limits.body
msgid "The score measures reliance, not quality. A young, excellent package can rank low, and a widely used one can still have bugs. Read the package page, its repository and its documentation before you depend on it."
msgstr ""

#: conf.json:strings.method.design
msgid "The full derivation in the score design notes"
msgstr ""

#: conf.json:strings.method.back
msgid "Back to the rankings"
msgstr ""

#: conf.json:strings.momentum.rule
msgid "A change counts as rising or cooling only when it reaches both 10 points and 10% of the earlier score."
msgstr ""
